BOOK = {
 "dir": "book1-cp",
 "files": {n: f"ch{n:02d}-cp.tex" for n in range(1, 19)},
 "ch": {
 1: ([r"Nothing beyond the \emph{Start here} chapter at the front of this book. If you have never compiled a C++ program, read its ``C++ in ten minutes'' section first; this chapter then treats the judge as a black box and teaches you to reason about it."],
     [r"explain what a judge does with your program and which of the six verdicts you earned, from the verdict alone;",
      r"submit a correct solution to a simple problem using the starter file, with fast input and 64-bit integers;",
      r"look at a problem's limits and say, before writing code, whether an $O(n^2)$ idea can pass;",
      r"run the read--test--submit loop and the first-Wrong-Answer checklist when a submission fails."]),
 2: ([r"Chapter~1: the starter file, \texttt{vector}, and the habit of checking the constraints before coding.",
      r"\emph{Recap:} a loop that tries every candidate costs (number of candidates)\,$\times$\,(cost per check). If that product is under about $10^8$, brute force is a legitimate answer, not a failure."],
     [r"write a complete-search solution for any problem whose answer space you can list, and estimate its size first;",
      r"enumerate all subsets with a bitmask and all orderings with \texttt{next\_permutation};",
      r"write a backtracking search and add the single prune that turns it from hopeless to fast;",
      r"tell, from the constraints, when brute force is the intended solution."]),
 3: ([r"Chapters~1--2. From \textbf{Book~2, Chapter~3} (induction) we borrow one idea: ``assume it works for smaller cases, show it works for the next.'' You do not need to have read that book.",
      r"\emph{Recap:} to \emph{prove} a greedy rule correct you must show that your first choice can be extended to an optimal answer. Arguing ``it feels right'' is how Wrong Answer is born."],
     [r"sort with a custom comparator and use sorting to turn a messy problem into a scan;",
      r"design a greedy rule and defend it with an exchange argument;",
      r"find a counterexample that kills a plausible-looking greedy in under five minutes;",
      r"use \texttt{set} and \texttt{map} to keep a changing collection ordered while you scan."]),
 4: ([r"Chapters~1--3. You should be comfortable writing a loop over a \texttt{vector} and reading a bound off a constraint.",
      r"\emph{Recap:} binary search needs a \emph{yes/no question that flips exactly once} as you move along a line. Finding that question is the whole skill; the code is ten lines."],
     [r"write one binary-search template that never loops forever and never is off by one;",
      r"turn ``find the smallest value that works'' into a binary search on the answer;",
      r"binary-search real numbers with a fixed iteration count instead of an epsilon;",
      r"recognise when a predicate is \emph{not} monotone and the pattern does not apply."]),
 5: ([r"Chapters~1 and~4. \emph{Recap:} an array query ``sum of $a_l\dots a_r$'' naively costs $O(r-l+1)$ per query; with $q$ queries on $n$ elements that is $O(nq)$, which fails when both are $2\cdot10^5$. This chapter removes the repeated work."],
     [r"answer any number of range-sum queries in $O(1)$ each after one $O(n)$ pass;",
      r"apply many range updates at once and read the final array with a difference array;",
      r"extend both ideas to a grid with inclusion--exclusion;",
      r"solve ``longest/shortest subarray with property $P$'' with two pointers instead of two nested loops."]),
 6: ([r"Chapters~1, 3 and~5. \emph{Recap:} \texttt{stack} means ``last in, first out''; \texttt{deque} means ``add or remove at either end.'' Think of a stack of plates (you only touch the top one) and a queue at a shop where you may also join or leave from the front. Both are one line of C++ (\texttt{stack<int>}, \texttt{deque<int>})."],
     [r"argue that a loop with a nested loop is still $O(n)$ because each element is pushed and popped once;",
      r"find the nearest smaller (or larger) element to the left of every position in one pass;",
      r"compute every sliding-window minimum in $O(n)$ with a deque;",
      r"build a sparse table and answer range-minimum queries in $O(1)$."]),
 7: ([r"Chapters~1 and~2. \emph{Recap:} a \emph{graph} is just a list of things (vertices) and a list of pairs that are connected (edges). A road map, a friendship network and the moves in a puzzle are all graphs. \textbf{Book~2, Chapter~11} studies graphs as mathematics; this chapter treats them as input to programs."],
     [r"store any graph as adjacency lists and read it from the standard input format;",
      r"traverse it with DFS and BFS and say which one a problem needs;",
      r"count connected components, flood-fill a grid and test for bipartiteness;",
      r"order a dependency graph with topological sort and detect a cycle."]),
 8: ([r"Chapter~7 (graph storage, BFS). \emph{Recap:} BFS finds the fewest-\emph{edges} path. Once edges carry costs (kilometres, minutes, fees) the fewest-edges path is no longer the cheapest path, and we need new tools."],
     [r"choose between BFS, Dijkstra, Bellman--Ford and Floyd--Warshall from the graph's size and edge weights;",
      r"write Dijkstra with a priority queue in under three minutes;",
      r"detect a negative cycle;",
      r"model a ``shortest path with extra rules'' problem by adding the rule to the vertex (state expansion)."]),
 9: ([r"Chapters~3 and~7. \emph{Recap:} a \emph{spanning tree} connects all $n$ vertices using $n-1$ edges and no cycles; the \emph{minimum} one has the smallest total edge cost. Kruskal's algorithm is a greedy algorithm (Chapter~3) run on a sorted edge list."],
     [r"keep track of groups that merge over time with a disjoint-set union in near-constant time per operation;",
      r"build a minimum spanning tree with Kruskal or Prim and explain why it is optimal;",
      r"answer offline connectivity questions by processing edges in the right order."]),
 10: ([r"Chapter~7 (DFS) and Chapter~9 (a tree is a connected graph with no cycle). \emph{Recap:} in a tree there is exactly one simple path between any two vertices, which is why tree problems are easier than general graph problems."],
      [r"root a tree and compute subtree sizes, depths and sums with one DFS;",
       r"find a tree's diameter with two DFS passes;",
       r"flatten a tree into an array with an Euler tour so range tools apply;",
       r"answer lowest-common-ancestor and path-distance queries with binary lifting."]),
 11: ([r"Chapters~2 and~5. \emph{Recap:} complete search (Chapter~2) can recompute the same sub-problem thousands of times. Dynamic programming is complete search plus a table that remembers answers; you will see this on a problem you have already solved."],
      [r"turn a recursive brute force into a table by naming the state and writing the transition;",
       r"solve coin-change, knapsack, longest-increasing-subsequence and grid-path problems;",
       r"decide whether a problem wants DP or a greedy;",
       r"recover the actual solution, not just its value, from a DP table."]),
 12: ([r"Chapter~11 (all of it) and Chapter~2 (bitmasks for subsets). \emph{Recap:} a DP \emph{state} is the smallest set of facts that determine the future. This chapter builds states out of bitmasks, digits, intervals and tree vertices."],
      [r"write a DP over subsets for problems with $n\le 20$;",
       r"count numbers in $[1,N]$ with a digit property by digit DP;",
       r"solve interval problems with range DP and tree problems with DP on a rooted tree;",
       r"recognise problems that need a DP optimisation you have not yet learned, and know where to look."]),
 13: ([r"Chapters~5 (prefix sums) and~6 (sparse tables). \emph{Recap:} prefix sums answer range sums but break when the array changes. Here the array \emph{changes} between queries, so we need a structure that supports update and query together in $O(\log n)$."],
      [r"build a Fenwick tree for point update, range sum;",
       r"build a segment tree for any associative combine (sum, min, max, gcd);",
       r"add lazy propagation for range updates;",
       r"pick the right structure for a given mix of updates and queries."]),
 14: ([r"Chapters~1 and~11. From \textbf{Book~2, Chapters~5--7} (divisibility, modular arithmetic, Fermat) we use the facts; the facts are restated in context here, so you can read this chapter without having read Book~2.",
       r"\emph{Recap:} $a \bmod m$ is the remainder of $a$ divided by $m$. Contest answers are often asked ``modulo $10^9+7$'' so that they fit in 64-bit integers."],
      [r"compute gcd, a prime sieve and a prime factorisation fast enough for $10^6$--$10^7$;",
       r"do addition, multiplication, power and division under a modulus without overflow;",
       r"compute $\binom{n}{k}\bmod p$ for many queries;",
       r"solve linear recurrences with matrix exponentiation."]),
 15: ([r"Chapters~1 and~14 (modular arithmetic). \emph{Recap:} a \emph{string} is an array of characters; a \emph{substring} is a contiguous piece; a \emph{prefix} is a substring that starts at position 0. Most string tools below answer ``does pattern $P$ occur in text $T$?'' in $O(|P|+|T|)$ instead of $O(|P||T|)$."],
      [r"compare two substrings in $O(1)$ with a rolling hash and say how likely a collision is;",
       r"find all occurrences of a pattern with KMP or the Z-function;",
       r"build a trie and use it to answer prefix questions;",
       r"decide between hashing, KMP, Z and a trie from the problem statement."]),
 16: ([r"Chapters~1 and~3 (sorting with comparators). \textbf{Book~3, Chapters~1 and~5} (vectors, dot products) is the same maths, with proofs; this chapter restates the two formulas you need, the dot and cross product."],
      [r"represent points as exact integers, so that no floating-point error can change an answer;",
       r"tell whether three points turn left, right, or are collinear with one cross product;",
       r"test whether two segments intersect and build a convex hull;",
       r"compute polygon area and decide whether a point is inside a polygon."]),
 17: ([r"Chapter~14 (modular arithmetic) and \textbf{Book~2, Chapter~12} (probability on finite spaces). \emph{Recap:} on a finite sample space with equally likely outcomes, $P(A)=|A|/|\Omega|$: probability is counting.",
       r"The expected value of a random quantity is the average you would see over many repetitions."],
      [r"turn a ``probability that\dots'' problem into a counting problem;",
       r"compute an expected value with linearity of expectation, without listing outcomes;",
       r"apply conditional probability and Bayes' rule to a short word problem;",
       r"compute a probability exactly with DP and know when to settle for simulation."]),
 18: ([r"All of the book up to Chapter~17 as a reference, but nothing new is required: this chapter is about \emph{process}. \emph{Recap:} the algorithms are the easy part of a contest; losing points to unread statements, untested code and badly managed time is what keeps most contestants below their real level."],
      [r"prepare a template, notes and environment before a contest starts;",
       r"allocate time among problems and know when to abandon one;",
       r"find a failing case by stress testing against a brute force;",
       r"run an upsolving routine that actually raises your rating."]),
 }
}
